[ROOT CAUSE GROUP 03] SESSION / TRANSACTION LIFECYCLE VIOLATIONS
  MODULE 05 set_state uses async with db.begin() then sets last_activity = func.now() (SQL expression object, not evaluated).
  MODULE 11 add_tokens / deduct_tokens: own_session path does async with db.begin() inside a for-retry loop while the session object is created outside; close happens in finally but begin() may leave the session in a dirty state on exception.
  MODULE 13 activate_package: nested begin() + with_for_update + OCC retry inside a single session that may already be in a transaction.
  MODULE 17B create_invoice: two separate sessions — one inserts payment_version=1, second attaches invoice; race window between them.
  MODULE 23 alby_webhook: multiple sequential begin() blocks on the same SessionLocal instance; after first begin the second begin raises InvalidRequestError or silently fails depending on driver.
  MODULE 26 recover_and_finalize_boot: opens many independent SessionLocal() contexts while the caller also holds a session; nested begin() + with_for_update across sessions produces lock contention and lost updates.
  Many places do db.get() then later db.execute(update) without ensuring the object is still attached or the transaction is open.

[ROOT CAUSE GROUP 04] OPTIMISTIC CONCURRENCY CONTROL (OCC) LOGIC ERRORS
  MODULE 11 _add_tokens_inner: for-loop over OCC_RETRIES, on success break, else after loop return False; however the payment finalization update is outside the OCC loop and can fail independently, leaving tokens credited but payment not marked.
  MODULE 11 deduct_tokens: isolation_level="SERIALIZABLE" set via execution_options on a select(1) which does not actually change the transaction isolation for subsequent statements in all SQLAlchemy/asyncpg versions.
  MODULE 13 activate_package: double OCC loop (outer attempt + inner retry) both using the same with_for_update row; version check can be stale between the two levels.
  MODULE 16 credit_referral: similar double OCC + referral_earned_today check; can overshoot DAILY_REF_CAP under concurrent credits.
  balance_version is incremented only on successful rowcount==1, but many call sites ignore the False return and proceed as if tokens moved.

[ROOT CAUSE GROUP 05] PAYMENT STATE MACHINE & VERSION GUARD INCONSISTENCIES
  Payment states: reserving / awaiting_payment / open / paid / dead. Multiple modules transition with different version numbers.
  MODULE 17B inserts state='awaiting_payment', payment_version=1.
  MODULE 23 webhook forces reserving→open or awaiting→open with version 1→2, then calls add_tokens which forces version 3 and paid=True.
  MODULE 26 recovery has three separate phases that re-read and re-write the same rows with overlapping version predicates; a payment can be marked dead in phase 2 while phase 3 still tries to credit it.
  UniqueConstraint uq_one_unpaid_invoice and Index uq_uid_payment_v1 use postgresql_where clauses that do not cover all transitional states, allowing two unpaid rows under race.
  CheckConstraint ck_state_mutation_authority and ck_invoice_single_writer are violated by recovery paths that write invoice_id while version is still 1.

[ROOT CAUSE GROUP 06] REDIS / IN-MEMORY LOCK & CACHE DIVERGENCE
  MODULE 11 maintains _uid_locks dict + Redis side-effect keys; locks are process-local only.
  MODULE 24A uid_lock decorator uses Redis NX lock with TTL; MODULE 24B lock_user_for_buy uses DB column invoice_lock_until.
  Three independent locking mechanisms (in-memory asyncio.Lock, Redis key, DB timestamp) never coordinate; concurrent buy + credit + package activation can interleave.
  Caches (_GATE_CACHE, _PREMIUM_CACHE, _REF_LINK_CACHE, _GIF_CACHE, _PRICE_CACHE, EXPIRING_SOON_SET) are process-local; multi-worker or restart loses them or serves stale data.
  MODULE 14 start_cache_clear spawns a task that clears caches but is never scheduled from MODULE 22 entry.
  MODULE 16 ref_links / _REF_LINK_CACHE recovered from Settings but never cleaned of expired entries except in a background loop that may not be started.

